# The issue
Some quantities are output at the previous/next timestep, rather than the current timestep. This depends on whether the GPU version of the code is used (note that in some cases, it is the CPU version that actually exhibits the wrong behaviour).

An offset of a single timestep is probably not an issue for production runs (where the output interval is typically much larger than the timestep), but can cause a lot of confusion if one is trying to validate the GPU version by comparing its outputs to the CPU version.

The output below is generated by running `python check_output_times.py` after `pc_run`

# On CPU
Snapshots and slices are output one timestep later than expected.
```
Time series:
        it:             [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        t:              [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialm:       [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Averages:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmz[:,0]:         [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmxy[:,0,0]:      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Snapshots:
        t:              [0.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0]
        special[0,0,0]: [0.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0]

Slices:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        xy.special[:,0,0]:      [1. 2. 3. 4. 5.]
```

# On GPU
(uncomment `GPU = gpu_astaroth` in src/Makefile.local)
Averages (both 1D and 2D) sometimes have wrong results
```
Time series:
        it:             [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        t:              [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialm:       [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Averages:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmz[:,0]:         [0.      1.      2.22222 3.49383 4.77641]
        specialmxy[:,0,0]:      [0.        1.        2.6666667 4.777778  7.185186 ]

Snapshots:
        t:              [0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]
        special[0,0,0]: [0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]

Slices:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        xy.special[:,0,0]:      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
```

#Configuration information (initial report)

Above is with
- gfortran 16.2.1
- openmp 23.1.1
- openmpi 5.0.11
- cmake 4.4.4
- bison 3.8.2
- flex 2.6.4
- sed 4.10
- python 3.14.7

Pencil config options are
```
%include compilers/GNU-GCC_MPI
%include compilers/extensions/GNU-GCC_large

%section Makefile
MAKE_VAR1 = -j7
FFLAGS += -fbacktrace -Og -g -finit-real=nan -finit-integer=-99999999 -fcheck=bounds -fcheck=bits -fcheck=do -fcheck=pointer -fmax-errors=1
ASTAROTH_CPU_BUILD = on
%endsection Makefile
```
